---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';

// About page. 
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="Who and why: a personal blog for learning and skill-building.">
  <div class="container section">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>About ObservedState.dev</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        ObservedState.dev is a personal learning notebook. It is where I practice, build skills and write down what I am
        working out about ServiceNow ITOM and the CMDB, and about the cloud, infrastructure as code, monitoring and AI
        that connect to them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Backing this site, and occasionally made visible through the writing, is an AWS environment and a ServiceNow personal
        development instance (PDI) where labs, learning and testing happen. In addition, when studying integrations, where
        I can, I will stand up and link those systems.
      </p>

      <h2>Who</h2>
      <p>
        I'm {SITE.author}, known by many as Rook. I have spent around 30 years in IT, the last 16 of them working with ServiceNow, and I hold
        the ServiceNow Certified Technical Architect (CTA) credential.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before I became a ServiceNow consultant, my work was in UNIX and Linux administration and in SOC/NOC Operations. That background is
        why I keep coming back to the same question: what is really running, and how do we know?
      </p>

      <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
      <p>
        That well-known platform that I have worked in now for 16 years sits next to a much larger world of cloud services and automation, 
        and the best way to keep learning is to build in it and write about it. I feel like the blog writing will force me to be precise, and a
        public blog keeps me honest. Most of what you will find here started as a learning exercise: a lab I built, something 
        that I wanted to understand better, or a finding worth mentioning.
      </p>
      <p>
        Already one of these learning processes is visible in how this site is built. It is a small static site on AWS, deployed 
        through a pipeline with no stored credentials, and the runbook for it is public in the
        <a href={SITE.github}>GitHub repository</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Today and the Professional Future</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>ServiceNow Certified Technical Architect (CTA)</strong>, is my main ServiceNow Expert-level credential.</li>
        <li>Servicenow CIS - Discovery</li>
        <li>Servicenow CIS - Event Management</li>
        <li>Servicenow CIS - Service Mapping</li>
        <li>Servicenow CIS - Data Foundations (CMDB/CSDM)</li>
        <li>Servicenow CIS - ITSM</li>
        <li>I do hold the ServiceNow Suite Certification for ITOM AIOps (all of them, there's a few).</li>
        <li>
          <strong>AWS Certified Solutions Architect &ndash; Associate</strong> and
          <strong>AWS Certified CloudOps Engineer &ndash; Associate</strong> (formerly SysOps Administrator). Both have
          lapsed, and part of what this site records is my path back to current AWS certifications.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        My approach to Certifications is to use them to structure and guide my learning, because I strongly believe that in the 
        real wold, simply having Certifications is not enough. I would rather show you a working lab or completed projects
        than a badge.  With that said, people search for Certifications in customer and recruiter conversations, so one can't just 
        ignore them. 
      </p>

      <h2>What this site is, and is not</h2>
      <p>
        ObservedState is a personal project for learning and professional development. It is <strong>not</strong> a
        consultancy, it does not offer services, and I do not take on work through it. The opinions here are mine alone
        and are not those of any employer or client. Nothing here describes any employer's or client's systems, data
        or plans: every example comes from my own labs, public documentation or generic patterns.
      </p>

      <h2>Why "observed state"</h2>
      <p>
        Infrastructure has a declared state (what the IaC template, the Change record or the architecture diagram says) and
        an observed state (what is actually found by running active commands against the device or environment). 
        The gap between these two things is drift, and closing it is what we call reconciliation.
        In Operations, the CMDB is where the drift shows up, where the reconciliation has to happen, and 
        this is why the concept of CMDB sits at the center of this site.
      </p>

      <h2>What you'll find here</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/writing/">Writing</a>: field notes, deep dives and teardowns.</li>
        <li><a href="/patterns/">Patterns</a>: a catalog of integration patterns on one template.</li>
        <li><a href="/paths/">Paths</a>: learning sequences for connecting ServiceNow to the cloud and beyond.</li>
        <li><a href="/drift-log/">Drift Log</a>: my own learning journal, including what did not go to plan.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
      <p>
        Code and examples will live on <a href={SITE.github}>GitHub</a>, and where applicable, linked from here.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>